  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "This variant fits the composition pressure well because it keeps the envelope transport-agnostic: peers see exact bytes plus a pCID dispatch boundary, and unsupported interpretations fail explicitly instead of leaking UDP-specific assumptions upward. It falls short of top marks because the draft does not define the UDP-feed-facing API, wrapper-order conventions, or broader malformed or hostile session cases.",
    "strengths": [
      "Very clear layer boundary: the envelope carries only pCID and opaque payload bytes.",
      "Unknown-pCID store/forward with explicit unsupported results supports sparse knowledge and forward compatibility.",
      "Deterministic CBOR bytes give stable hashing and signing inputs for later audit."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "No explicit mapping from UDP-feed v0 events or API to the next session layer.",
      "Auditability depends on external handler and signature-wrapper specs; unknown payloads stay opaque.",
      "Session semantics such as ordering, membership, and retransmission are delegated but not illustrated."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Wrapper-only signatures may create ambiguity about what the session layer must verify and in what order.",
      "Unfrozen pCID and spec lifecycle could weaken long-term interoperability.",
      "Opaque forwarding can preserve bytes while delaying policy checks until a handler is available."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "What exact UDP-feed v0 API surface is required so the next layer can preserve byte identity without transport leakage?",
      "What canonical wrapper ordering is expected when signatures, hop evidence, and session payloads are nested?",
      "How should malformed CBOR, excessive nesting, or conflicting wrappers fail and be recorded locally?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
